  "context": [
    "The current Camry generation (XV70) was reintroduced to India in 2019 exclusively as a self-charging hybrid, after Toyota discontinued the plain-petrol Camry that had been sold here since the early 2000s, making it one of the first full-hybrid sedans sold at scale in the country.",
    "Sold in essentially a single, fully-loaded trim throughout its run, the Camry's buyer base is almost entirely corporate fleets, senior executives, and chauffeur-driven owners rather than self-driven retail buyers, which keeps odometer readings on used examples unusually low relative to the car's age.",
    "Toyota's hybrid reliability reputation, built over more than a decade of Camry and Prius hybrid systems globally, gives the car unusually strong percentage retention for a low-volume premium sedan, but the very small number of cars sold each year means the resale market is thin and a buyer may take longer to find than for a higher-volume luxury sedan.",
    "Because the Camry has no petrol-only or diesel sibling in India, every used Camry on the market is a hybrid, which removes the powertrain-choice complexity that complicates resale comparisons for most other sedans in this bracket, such as the Mercedes-Benz E-Class or Honda City."
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of a Toyota Camry in India?",
      "a": "A Toyota Camry Hybrid retains roughly 76% of its ex-showroom price after two years and about 68% after three years, still around 56% at year five, well above typical premium sedan retention as of September 2026."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the Camry hold value better than most sedans in its price range?",
      "a": "Its 3-year retention of about 68% sits roughly 10 points above the Sedan segment average of 58%, a combination of Toyota's hybrid reliability reputation and low-mileage usage patterns from its mostly chauffeur-driven, corporate-fleet ownership base."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is it hard to resell a Toyota Camry in India?",
      "a": "The percentage value retained is strong, but the Camry's very low sales volumes mean the pool of interested used-car buyers is small, so sellers should expect a longer time-to-sale than a higher-volume luxury sedan, even at a favourable price."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does a Camry depreciate per year?",
      "a": "A Camry Hybrid loses about 15% of value in year one, with 3-5 percentage point annual retention drops through year five (to roughly 56%), a notably flatter curve than the sedan segment average of 45% at the same age."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale price for a Camry?",
      "a": "Delhi NCR and Mumbai trade around +2% above the national mid, reflecting the largest pools of executive and chauffeur-driven buyers, while Kolkata trades roughly -2% because of a very limited premium hybrid-sedan market there."
    }
